      {"type":"mc","q":"A class must distinguish a repeating annual temperature pattern from daily variation. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?","choices":["One warm winter day erases the annual pattern","Seasonal temperature data usually show warmer and cooler parts of the year, although daily values vary","Temperature never varies within a season","Every summer day is warmer than every winter day"],"answer":"Seasonal temperature data usually show warmer and cooler parts of the year, although daily values vary","explain":"Seasonal temperature data usually show warmer and cooler parts of the year, although daily values vary This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge.","audio":"A class must distinguish a repeating annual temperature pattern from daily variation. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Two years of same-date measurements show nearly the same rise-and-fall pattern. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?","choices":["Only descriptive words can show seasonal change","Measurements collected consistently across many dates reveal seasonal patterns more reliably than one observation","Changing instruments improves comparison","One afternoon is enough to establish an annual pattern"],"answer":"Measurements collected consistently across many dates reveal seasonal patterns more reliably than one observation","explain":"Measurements collected consistently across many dates reveal seasonal patterns more reliably than one observation This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge.","audio":"Two years of same-date measurements show nearly the same rise-and-fall pattern. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"True or false: “A repeated sequence in past temperature and daylight data can support a prediction for the next season” correctly answers “A model must predict the likely direction of daylight change after the shortest days of winter. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion.”","choices":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explain":"The statement is true. A repeated sequence in past temperature and daylight data can support a prediction for the next season This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge.","audio":"True or false: “A repeated sequence in past temperature and daylight data can support a prediction for the next season” correctly answers “A model must predict the likely direction of daylight change after the shortest days of winter. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion.”"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which answer correctly resolves “A data table ends with autumn and asks for the next two seasons in the repeating sequence. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion” and replaces the incorrect choice “The seasons occur in a random order”?","choices":["The seasons occur in a random order","A season sequence happens only once","The seasons repeat in the order spring, summer, autumn, winter, and then spring again","Winter is always followed by autumn"],"answer":"The seasons repeat in the order spring, summer, autumn, winter, and then spring again","explain":"The seasons repeat in the order spring, summer, autumn, winter, and then spring again is the defensible answer. The seasons repeat in the order spring, summer, autumn, winter, and then spring again This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge. The response \"The seasons occur in a random order\" fails at least one condition, so the correction uses the complete sci lesson evidence.","audio":"Which answer correctly resolves “A data table ends with autumn and asks for the next two seasons in the repeating sequence. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion” and replaces the incorrect choice “The seasons occur in a random order”?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which answer to “Monthly daylight records rise from January to June and then decline toward December. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion” is supported by all relevant evidence rather than “Daylight changes randomly from month to month”?","choices":["Daylight changes randomly from month to month","Winter always has the longest days","In a yearly local pattern, daylight generally lengthens toward summer and shortens toward winter","Every season has exactly twelve hours of daylight"],"answer":"In a yearly local pattern, daylight generally lengthens toward summer and shortens toward winter","explain":"In a yearly local pattern, daylight generally lengthens toward summer and shortens toward winter is the defensible answer. In a yearly local pattern, daylight generally lengthens toward summer and shortens toward winter This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge. The response \"Daylight changes randomly from month to month\" fails at least one condition, so the correction uses the complete sci lesson evidence.","audio":"Which answer to “Monthly daylight records rise from January to June and then decline toward December. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion” is supported by all relevant evidence rather than “Daylight changes randomly from month to month”?"}
