      {"type":"mc","q":"One measurement differs greatly from four similar measurements. Which conclusion best applies repeated trials and revision?","choices":["Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements","A scientist should remove every unexpected result","Repeated trials guarantee a preferred answer","Revision means changing data to match a claim"],"answer":"Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements","explain":"Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements The evidence in this situation matches repeated trials and revision.","audio":"One measurement differs greatly from four similar measurements. Which conclusion best applies repeated trials and revision?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"A class is evaluating testable questions. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence?","choices":["A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements","A scientific question must have a predetermined answer","Every question requires no evidence","A testable question asks only for a personal preference"],"answer":"A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements","explain":"A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements The other choices conflict with the observable pattern or system evidence.","audio":"A class is evaluating testable questions. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"True or false: “A fair comparison changes the tested variable while keeping other relevant conditions consistent” correctly answers “A class is evaluating controlled variables. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence.”","choices":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explain":"The statement is true. A fair comparison changes the tested variable while keeping other relevant conditions consistent The other choices conflict with the observable pattern or system evidence.","audio":"True or false: “A fair comparison changes the tested variable while keeping other relevant conditions consistent” correctly answers “A class is evaluating controlled variables. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence.”"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"A class is evaluating organized data. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence?","choices":["Tables and graphs organize measurements so features, differences, and patterns can be identified","Data order cannot affect interpretation","A memory of the results is stronger than recorded data","Graphs should omit units and labels"],"answer":"Tables and graphs organize measurements so features, differences, and patterns can be identified","explain":"Tables and graphs organize measurements so features, differences, and patterns can be identified The other choices conflict with the observable pattern or system evidence.","audio":"A class is evaluating organized data. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"A class is evaluating evidence-based conclusions. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence?","choices":["A conclusion should repeat the prediction even when data disagree","A valid conclusion answers the question using the collected data and states the limits of the evidence","Opinions are stronger than measurements","One result supports every possible situation"],"answer":"A valid conclusion answers the question using the collected data and states the limits of the evidence","explain":"A valid conclusion answers the question using the collected data and states the limits of the evidence The other choices conflict with the observable pattern or system evidence.","audio":"A class is evaluating evidence-based conclusions. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"A class is evaluating repeated trials and revision. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence?","choices":["Revision means changing data to match a claim","Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements","Repeated trials guarantee a preferred answer","A scientist should remove every unexpected result"],"answer":"Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements","explain":"Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements The other choices conflict with the observable pattern or system evidence.","audio":"A class is evaluating repeated trials and revision. Which claim is supported by the strongest Grade 4 science evidence?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"True or false: “A testable question asks only for a personal preference” correctly answers “A class revises 'Which ramp is best?' into a measurable comparison with defined conditions. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion.”","choices":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explain":"The statement is false. A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge. The correct answer is \"A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements,\" not \"A testable question asks only for a personal preference.\"","audio":"True or false: “A testable question asks only for a personal preference” correctly answers “A class revises 'Which ramp is best?' into a measurable comparison with defined conditions. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion.”"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Two plant-light treatments require equal species, soil, water, pot size, and observation time. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?","choices":["A fair comparison changes all conditions at once","Controlled variables are the final results","Repeating a trial replaces the need for controls","A fair comparison changes the tested variable while keeping other relevant conditions consistent"],"answer":"A fair comparison changes the tested variable while keeping other relevant conditions consistent","explain":"A fair comparison changes the tested variable while keeping other relevant conditions consistent This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge.","audio":"Two plant-light treatments require equal species, soil, water, pot size, and observation time. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"A graph must reveal whether distance consistently rises as height rises and show an unusual trial. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?","choices":["Tables and graphs organize measurements so features, differences, and patterns can be identified","Graphs should omit units and labels","A memory of the results is stronger than recorded data","Data order cannot affect interpretation"],"answer":"Tables and graphs organize measurements so features, differences, and patterns can be identified","explain":"Tables and graphs organize measurements so features, differences, and patterns can be identified This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge.","audio":"A graph must reveal whether distance consistently rises as height rises and show an unusual trial. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Data support a pattern in one car-and-surface setup but do not test all vehicles or surfaces. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?","choices":["A valid conclusion answers the question using the collected data and states the limits of the evidence","One result supports every possible situation","A conclusion should repeat the prediction even when data disagree","Opinions are stronger than measurements"],"answer":"A valid conclusion answers the question using the collected data and states the limits of the evidence","explain":"A valid conclusion answers the question using the collected data and states the limits of the evidence This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge.","audio":"Data support a pattern in one car-and-surface setup but do not test all vehicles or surfaces. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"True or false: “Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements” correctly answers “A class checks the procedure, repeats the trial, and revises the model when new evidence remains different. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion.”","choices":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explain":"The statement is true. Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge.","audio":"True or false: “Repeated trials reveal consistency, reduce the influence of unusual results, and can guide improvements” correctly answers “A class checks the procedure, repeats the trial, and revises the model when new evidence remains different. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion.”"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which answer correctly resolves “A class revises 'Which ramp is best?' into a measurable comparison with defined conditions. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion” and replaces the incorrect choice “A testable question asks only for a personal preference”?","choices":["A testable question asks only for a personal preference","A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements","A scientific question must have a predetermined answer","Every question requires no evidence"],"answer":"A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements","explain":"A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements is the defensible answer. A scientific investigation begins with a question that can be answered using observations or measurements This conclusion accounts for every condition in the challenge. The response \"A testable question asks only for a personal preference\" fails at least one condition, so the correction uses the complete sci lesson evidence.","audio":"Which answer correctly resolves “A class revises 'Which ramp is best?' into a measurable comparison with defined conditions. Which reasoning produces the most defensible conclusion” and replaces the incorrect choice “A testable question asks only for a personal preference”?"}
